## Final Judgement
## Summary of Key Findings

The evidence from the fact-check reveals several critical points. First, the factual premises of the claim—that Pogba played for Manchester United and the French national team—are confirmed. Second, Macron did make comments about Islamist terrorism in October 2020 following the beheading of Samuel Paty. However, the central assertion of the claim—that Pogba retired from international football in response to those comments—is directly and explicitly refuted. The retirement story originated from 195sports (the same source as the claim) and was picked up only by The Sun, while Pogba himself publicly denounced the reports as "unacceptable fake news" and "100% Fake News," vowing legal action against those spreading the rumor. Furthermore, Pogba remained available for and continued to represent the French national team after October 2020, and no credible major outlet (BBC, ESPN, L'Équipe, Reuters) ever confirmed the retirement.

## Information Still Lacking

No additional information is needed. The fact-check provides comprehensive and clear evidence, including Pogba's own direct denial of the claim and his continued participation with the national team, which is sufficient to reach a definitive conclusion.

## Conclusion

The claim is clearly refuted by the evidence. Pogba himself explicitly denied retiring from international football, the story was labeled as fake news, and he continued to be available for the French national team. The claim's core assertion is contradicted both by Pogba's own statements and by the absence of any credible confirmation.

